1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Our passport to the deep.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Underwater breathing inventions that allow us to invade inner space.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
To ride in aquatic chariots.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:16,000
To explore the dangers and mysteries beneath the waves.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, scuba and deep sea diving on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It is one of the most dramatic and memorable moments in underwater history.

7
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:51,660
Sylvia Earle, undersea's pioneer and explorer,

8
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:55,780
dons an 800-pound pressurized atmospheric suit

9
00:00:55,781 --> 00:00:58,220
to dive straight down to the ocean floor.

10
00:00:58,221 --> 00:01:02,020
1,250 feet.

11
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:05,220
She descends to a dark and alien world

12
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:09,460
where underwater pressure is 600 pounds per square inch.

13
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,500
Only her armored suit keeps her body

14
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,340
from being utterly crushed.

15
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:20,040
At about 700 feet, it was truly that sort of violet blue,

16
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:22,480
this ultra blue.

17
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:23,640
And you could see lights.

18
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:25,020
You could see bioluminescence.

19
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:28,220
It's like falling into stars.

20
00:01:28,221 --> 00:01:32,820
More men have walked on the moon than where this woman walked.

21
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:36,960
And untethered and alone, she became the first human being

22
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:39,760
ever to make a solo dive at this depth.

23
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:46,400
Sylvia Earle's historic dive was in 1979.

24
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:50,740
And even since then, less than 1% of our planet's ocean depths

25
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:54,320
have been seen by man, much less explored.

26
00:01:54,321 --> 00:01:58,020
It has been a struggle to penetrate an unnatural world

27
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,800
where we cannot breathe without help.

28
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:05,800
It is also a world that poses a troublesome problem of pressure.

29
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:08,840
Water is 800 times as dense as air.

30
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:11,800
And when descending below sea level, pressure increases

31
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:13,000
rapidly.

32
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:15,940
At about 400 feet down, your body is subjected

33
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:19,740
to almost 177 pounds of pressure.

34
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,240
Beyond a certain depth, your body would be crushed

35
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:24,320
by the force of the water.

36
00:02:24,321 --> 00:02:28,220
But the lure, the siren song, to dive deep has always

37
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,360
attracted us.

38
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:32,220
Man's primordial desire to go below the waves

39
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:34,960
began even before recorded time.

40
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:37,040
Even then, he knew there had to be

41
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:42,900
a way to overcome the natural limits under the sea.

42
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,840
It was our first real breathing device, the hollow reed.

43
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:50,940
Ancient man probably got the idea for the snorkel

44
00:02:50,941 --> 00:02:54,880
from watching mammoths use their trunks to breathe underwater.

45
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:57,680
It only worked to the depth of about a foot and a half.

46
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:00,680
But if he had a sack of air, then he

47
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:04,180
could take his air supply with him.

48
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:07,620
This diver from 2,000 years ago breathes

49
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:09,920
from an inflated animal bladder.

50
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:12,200
It was only possible up to about 20 feet,

51
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:15,040
because water pressure would compress the sack of air,

52
00:03:15,140 --> 00:03:17,440
squeezing it almost flat.

53
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:20,140
But it was the start.

54
00:03:20,141 --> 00:03:24,320
In a stunning illustration recounting a scene in 332 BC,

55
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:26,840
Alexander the Great descends into the sea

56
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:30,720
in a great glass barrel called the Klympha.

57
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,880
He was said to have seen marine monsters

58
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:34,980
and a species of demon.

59
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,020
If Alexander's descent was true, it

60
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:41,260
was in the first diving bell, a milestone in man's attempt

61
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:44,000
to explore the sea.

62
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:46,900
But it wasn't until the dawn of the 16th century

63
00:03:46,901 --> 00:03:52,200
that the age of invention for diving really began.

64
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:57,340
It's herald, artist, inventor, and visionary, Leonardo da

65
00:03:57,440 --> 00:03:58,140
Vinci.

66
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:05,260
At the time, Venice was locked in bitter battle against the Turks

67
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:08,020
and had hatched a plan to use divers to sabotage

68
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:09,260
Turkish ships.

69
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:11,920
They wanted Leonardo to invent a breathing device

70
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:14,020
to help win the war.

71
00:04:14,021 --> 00:04:19,020
There's no possible way you could suck air more than about 18 inches.

72
00:04:19,021 --> 00:04:21,020
So it was great on the paper.

73
00:04:21,021 --> 00:04:23,020
He even made what looked like the first scuba equipment,

74
00:04:23,021 --> 00:04:26,020
an inflated bladder with the mouthpiece on and all that.

75
00:04:26,021 --> 00:04:28,020
And that would be great in a couple feet of water.

76
00:04:28,021 --> 00:04:31,020
But remember, as you go down, it would get compressed, compressed, compressed.

77
00:04:31,021 --> 00:04:34,020
And you'd only have a few breaths, let's say, at 30 feet.

78
00:04:34,021 --> 00:04:39,020
So none of his things really, really work, but they're beautiful to look at.

79
00:04:39,021 --> 00:04:43,020
Like other science fiction-type snorkels designed during the Renaissance,

80
00:04:43,021 --> 00:04:46,860
Leonardo's would only have worked to a depth of about a foot and a half.

81
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:50,120
A diver's lungs would be subject to pressures so great,

82
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:53,860
he'd be unable to draw air down for more than a few breaths.

83
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:57,060
Treasure.

84
00:04:58,200 --> 00:04:59,640
Gold.

85
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:00,880
Jewels.

86
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:03,100
Pieces of eight.

87
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:08,440
War wasn't the only reason why diving tools were becoming a necessity.

88
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:12,080
Every year that passed, the need grew to dive down deep,

89
00:05:12,081 --> 00:05:14,480
since Columbus' discovery of the New World,

90
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:19,360
ships laden with riches sunk from storms and pirates.

91
00:05:19,460 --> 00:05:22,320
So busy inventors turned to the diving bell

92
00:05:22,420 --> 00:05:24,300
to recover riches.

93
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:26,020
Made of metal wood or leather, they

94
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:28,200
trapped air from the surface.

95
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:30,700
And when held level as they descended,

96
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:33,700
the air was kept from escaping.

97
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:37,440
They enclosed either a diver's head or entire body.

98
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:40,800
It was an air bank for workers who would swim outside,

99
00:05:40,801 --> 00:05:46,240
get their job done, and return when they needed a gulp of air.

100
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:48,640
The most successful bells were created

101
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:52,940
by the famous English astronomer Dr. Edmund Haley.

102
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:55,980
He conceived of air being lowered down in casks

103
00:05:56,080 --> 00:05:58,960
to replenish stagnant, sometimes poisonous air.

104
00:05:59,060 --> 00:06:01,460
And his new bell found plenty of uses.

105
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:04,360
The most common use for the bell was on salvage

106
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:07,900
or construction of harbors and inspection,

107
00:06:07,901 --> 00:06:10,100
sometimes doing bridge work.

108
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:13,640
But generally, the ones that are recorded in the early days

109
00:06:13,641 --> 00:06:14,940
are salvage bells.

110
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:20,080
But it wasn't until the invention of a reliable pump in 1788

111
00:06:20,081 --> 00:06:22,780
that diving bells really became commonplace.

112
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:26,860
Now, continuous fresh air could be pumped from the surface,

113
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:29,160
and the stagnant air could escape underneath.

114
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:32,440
This innovation made such an improvement in bells

115
00:06:32,441 --> 00:06:34,440
that by the end of the 18th century,

116
00:06:34,441 --> 00:06:36,940
few big ports were without them.

117
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:48,360
Of all things, it was fire that helped inventors enter the new age of diving.

118
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:56,460
Fire and two brothers, Charles and John Dean of Wextable, England, together they created the diving helmet.

119
00:06:56,960 --> 00:07:01,900
They actually evolved from a firefighting helmet, smoke helmet they called it.

120
00:07:01,901 --> 00:07:10,460
The Dean brothers were firefighters, and they lived in England, and they came up with a way to go into a burning house to rescue people.

121
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:16,320
And they thought, gee, this might work for going underwater and doing salvage, and it did, of course.

122
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:25,280
The Dean brothers teamed up with a man named Augustus Seabee to refine the smoke helmet, and what they came up with was the first open diving suit.

123
00:07:25,281 --> 00:07:31,180
It had a brass helmet with air pumped in from the surface, and excess air vented out near the waste.

124
00:07:31,181 --> 00:07:34,020
It was a very, very dangerous piece of equipment

125
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:37,320
because you could not lean forward too far

126
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:40,220
because the water level would come up as you lent.

127
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:43,720
And you certainly couldn't lay down because it would flood.

128
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:46,860
In 1837, Seabee modified his suit,

129
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:50,160
making it completely waterproof and adding an exhaust vent

130
00:07:50,260 --> 00:07:51,860
on top of the helmet.

131
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:55,300
Suddenly, the diver walked freely on the ocean floor

132
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:57,740
and breathed air from the surface.

133
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:01,180
The helmet had replaced the bell.

134
00:08:01,181 --> 00:08:03,180
Remember, if you're using a diving bell,

135
00:08:03,181 --> 00:08:05,180
you're only down for a small period of time,

136
00:08:05,181 --> 00:08:07,180
then you've got to try to go back and find that place

137
00:08:07,181 --> 00:08:09,180
where you just grabbed a handful of gold coins.

138
00:08:09,181 --> 00:08:11,180
If you've got a helmet suit on, man,

139
00:08:11,181 --> 00:08:14,180
you could keep filling sack after sack with those gold coins.

140
00:08:14,181 --> 00:08:15,180
A lot more fun.

141
00:08:15,181 --> 00:08:18,180
As soon as Seabee's new closed helmet and suit

142
00:08:18,181 --> 00:08:21,180
could be manufactured, it was put to the test

143
00:08:21,181 --> 00:08:23,180
on the wreck of the Royal George.

144
00:08:23,181 --> 00:08:26,180
Sunk 50 years before, the Royal George had been the biggest,

145
00:08:26,181 --> 00:08:30,180
most important ship the British Navy had ever seen.

146
00:08:30,181 --> 00:08:34,020
the British Navy owned, but now it served as a serious barrier

147
00:08:34,021 --> 00:08:36,380
in the busy port at Spithead.

148
00:08:36,381 --> 00:08:40,720
Lying 65 feet down, diving bells had been tried,

149
00:08:40,721 --> 00:08:43,320
but strong currents made them useless.

150
00:08:43,321 --> 00:08:46,860
The Seabee suit was a huge success.

151
00:08:46,861 --> 00:08:50,840
With the Seabee helmet that was introduced onto the Royal George

152
00:08:50,841 --> 00:08:55,380
in 1840 and was modified slightly during that season,

153
00:08:55,381 --> 00:08:57,980
you had basically perfection.

154
00:08:57,981 --> 00:09:00,120
I mean, the only additions that really happened with his suit

155
00:09:00,121 --> 00:09:02,140
is, you know, after World War I,

156
00:09:02,141 --> 00:09:04,680
they finally put a microphone where

157
00:09:04,681 --> 00:09:06,220
you could talk to the guys on the surface

158
00:09:06,221 --> 00:09:08,700
and they could talk to you, and a few refinements.

159
00:09:08,701 --> 00:09:11,980
But basically, for 150 years, his suit was still in use.

160
00:09:11,981 --> 00:09:14,640
And it's still in use in a lot of places in the world today.

161
00:09:14,641 --> 00:09:19,140
It took 5 and 1 half years to salvage the Royal George.

162
00:09:19,141 --> 00:09:22,620
But single-handedly, it launched a new technology,

163
00:09:22,621 --> 00:09:24,420
commercial diving.

164
00:09:24,421 --> 00:09:27,440
And as the helmet diver found more uses for his suit,

165
00:09:27,441 --> 00:09:29,740
And with improvements in air compressors,

166
00:09:29,741 --> 00:09:33,320
he began to descend deeper and deeper.

167
00:09:33,321 --> 00:09:36,680
And that's when he discovered the bends.

168
00:09:36,681 --> 00:09:38,220
If you're a commercial diver, you

169
00:09:38,221 --> 00:09:41,080
have to have the expectation that you are going to get bent.

170
00:09:41,081 --> 00:09:42,620
It's not if you're going to get bent,

171
00:09:42,621 --> 00:09:44,460
it's when you're going to get bent.

172
00:09:44,461 --> 00:09:47,600
When a diver descends, the amount of nitrogen dissolved

173
00:09:47,601 --> 00:09:49,920
in his body increases proportionally

174
00:09:49,921 --> 00:09:52,160
to the increase in water pressure.

175
00:09:52,161 --> 00:09:55,460
At 33 feet down, he breathes twice as much nitrogen

176
00:09:55,461 --> 00:09:56,940
as on the surface.

177
00:09:56,941 --> 00:10:01,040
at 66 feet down, three times as much, and so on.

178
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:03,540
And when he's been breathing compressed air for some time

179
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:07,760
at some depth and then rises, the nitrogen in his blood

180
00:10:07,860 --> 00:10:11,020
forms bubbles as the pressure decreases,

181
00:10:11,120 --> 00:10:14,720
like a cork being popped on a champagne bottle.

182
00:10:14,820 --> 00:10:16,520
These bubbles can land in the joints

183
00:10:16,620 --> 00:10:20,300
and create pain in the elbows or shoulder, or worse,

184
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:24,000
they can lodge in the spinal column and cause paralysis.

185
00:10:24,100 --> 00:10:25,660
They can even get stuck in the lungs

186
00:10:25,661 --> 00:10:28,460
or accumulate in the heart and cause death.

187
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:31,800
In the endeavor to find a solution to the problem,

188
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:35,600
a Scotsman, John Haldane, conceived of decompression

189
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:38,700
tables in 1907.

190
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:43,240
The tables specify waiting stages for ascending divers, stops

191
00:10:43,340 --> 00:10:46,380
they have to make on the way up to allow nitrogen

192
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:48,720
to pass through their system.

193
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:52,160
So now, with an unlimited supply of air,

194
00:10:52,260 --> 00:10:54,560
a way to avoid the bends, and clear

195
00:10:54,561 --> 00:10:57,400
communication from a line from your tender.

196
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:03,060
There was almost nothing helmet divers couldn't do except swim.

197
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:06,340
It took inventors of the self-contained underwater

198
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:10,480
breathing apparatus to find that kind of freedom in the sea

199
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:15,820
and a better way to battle enemies beneath the waves.

200
00:11:15,920 --> 00:11:19,320
16th century eyewitness accounts of the Lucayan Indians

201
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:21,220
of the Bahamas reported that they

202
00:11:21,221 --> 00:11:23,680
that they were able to dive to 100 feet

203
00:11:23,780 --> 00:11:26,660
and stay down as long as 15 minutes.

204
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:31,060
The divers believe they owed their endurance to tobacco.

205
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:34,560
Scuba and Deep Sea Diving will return on Modern Marvels.

206
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:41,700
We now return to Scuba and Deep Sea Diving

207
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:42,800
on Modern Marvels.

208
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:50,720
It was Jules Verne's turn of the century science fiction dream.

209
00:11:50,721 --> 00:11:55,700
Men walking on the floor of the sea, untethered, free as a fish,

210
00:11:55,800 --> 00:12:00,660
and shooting sharks with underwater rifles,

211
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:02,360
and battling giant squid.

212
00:12:07,940 --> 00:12:10,540
But this was no more strange and fanciful

213
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:14,280
than the first workable full-time, self-contained,

214
00:12:14,380 --> 00:12:18,220
underwater breathing apparatus, or scuba.

215
00:12:18,221 --> 00:12:21,320
It was a suit made for walking, not swimming.

216
00:12:21,420 --> 00:12:25,000
Created in 1825 by Englishman William James,

217
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:29,160
it was a huge air reservoir worn like a belt around the waist.

218
00:12:29,260 --> 00:12:31,760
And instead of fins, it came with boots,

219
00:12:31,860 --> 00:12:34,700
keeping divers' feet firmly planted on the bottom.

220
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:40,580
The idea for the next big step in the development of scuba

221
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:42,380
came not from the sea, but from deep

222
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:46,720
within the black pits of coal mines.

223
00:12:46,721 --> 00:12:50,860
In the 1860s, miners faced horrible dangers, one

224
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:53,160
of the worst, methane gas.

225
00:12:53,260 --> 00:12:55,600
Deadly and odorless, miners couldn't

226
00:12:55,700 --> 00:12:58,200
be rescued from the fumes because of the danger

227
00:12:58,300 --> 00:13:00,540
to the rescuer.

228
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:03,640
But French mining engineer Benoit Rucayrol

229
00:13:03,740 --> 00:13:07,500
and Navy officer Lieutenant Auguste Dennerouz changed that.

230
00:13:11,540 --> 00:13:14,540
Invented for miners but adapted for divers,

231
00:13:14,541 --> 00:13:20,260
the Rukai Roll-Deniru's apparatus actually depended upon air pumped down from the surface

232
00:13:20,261 --> 00:13:23,080
to a metal cylinder strapped on the diver's back.

233
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:28,760
But it could be disconnected for short bursts using just the air in the tank.

234
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:32,980
The restriction of it, why it wasn't hugely successful,

235
00:13:33,140 --> 00:13:39,180
was they didn't have the technology to pressurize the air cylinders to any great degree.

236
00:13:39,181 --> 00:13:43,660
The original units would hold somewhere about eight or nine

237
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:45,460
breaths, somewhere around there.

238
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:48,460
But what really earned these Frenchmen's invention

239
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:51,100
an immortal place in the history of diving

240
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:53,720
was their regulator, a piece of equipment

241
00:13:53,820 --> 00:13:56,540
that was more important than any other in the development

242
00:13:56,640 --> 00:13:58,300
of modern scuba.

243
00:13:58,400 --> 00:14:01,360
It was air on demand.

244
00:14:01,460 --> 00:14:03,880
The regulator was an attachment to the tank

245
00:14:03,980 --> 00:14:06,800
that controlled the flow of air passing from the hose

246
00:14:06,801 --> 00:14:08,840
to the diver's mouthpiece.

247
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:11,100
When air pressure inside the air reservoir

248
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:13,040
was greater than the water pressure,

249
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:16,340
a membrane automatically sealed off the valve.

250
00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:19,920
When the diver inhaled, air pressure decreased quickly,

251
00:14:20,020 --> 00:14:25,020
causing the valve to open, providing more air.

252
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:28,320
Just five years after Ruqairold Dennerouz

253
00:14:28,420 --> 00:14:31,760
unveiled their engineering feat, it was immortalized

254
00:14:31,860 --> 00:14:34,420
in Jules Verne's masterpiece of fiction,

255
00:14:34,421 --> 00:14:37,860
20,000 leagues under the sea.

256
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:40,620
Readers thought the author was a genius

257
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:43,160
and were amazed at his flights of fancy.

258
00:14:43,260 --> 00:14:47,560
Little did they realize it wasn't fiction at all.

259
00:14:47,660 --> 00:14:48,860
He didn't have to create anything.

260
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:51,480
It was in the trade catalogs of Rucao Denaru.

261
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:54,280
It's all available in France and elsewhere.

262
00:14:54,380 --> 00:14:57,680
And all he had to do was give him an unlimited air supply.

263
00:14:57,780 --> 00:15:01,280
So the fact enforces the fiction.

264
00:15:01,380 --> 00:15:02,420
Great.

265
00:15:02,520 --> 00:15:03,680
Perfect.

266
00:15:03,681 --> 00:15:05,720
The next milestone in man's effort

267
00:15:05,820 --> 00:15:09,020
toward becoming self-contained is the first fully independent

268
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:14,020
diving lung, the rebreather, first engineered in 1878.

269
00:15:14,120 --> 00:15:16,920
It functioned on air regeneration.

270
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:19,300
Instead of using compressed air, the rebreather

271
00:15:19,400 --> 00:15:20,800
used pure oxygen.

272
00:15:20,900 --> 00:15:23,660
And at the heart of the invention was its filter.

273
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:26,040
Filled with caustic soda, it absorbed

274
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:29,640
all the dangerous carbon dioxide exhausted by the diver,

275
00:15:29,740 --> 00:15:33,680
so he could breathe the same oxygen over and over again.

276
00:15:33,681 --> 00:15:36,780
Not only could you stay down longer with the rebreather,

277
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:39,780
but there were also no telltale bubbles

278
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:41,220
released in the water.

279
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:45,420
It was an amazing invention, but potentially a killer.

280
00:15:45,520 --> 00:15:48,320
Below about 30 feet, oxygen can be poisonous,

281
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:51,240
causing brain cells to fire at random,

282
00:15:51,340 --> 00:15:53,640
triggering convulsions underwater,

283
00:15:53,740 --> 00:15:56,140
and worse, result in drowning.

284
00:15:56,240 --> 00:15:59,640
But then the evolution of the underwater breathing apparatus

285
00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:01,340
stops for a few decades.

286
00:16:01,341 --> 00:16:05,340
In fact, until the early 1930s, when in the warm waters

287
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:09,980
of the Mediterranean, recreational diving was launched.

288
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:13,080
It was a new adventure, spear hunting.

289
00:16:13,180 --> 00:16:15,380
And with just goggles, fins, and a snorkel,

290
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:20,400
it attracted new enthusiasts to a new idea, diving for fun.

291
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:23,120
A young Jacques Cousteau was one of the new gogglers,

292
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:26,700
and so was another Frenchman, Commandant Yves Le Priere,

293
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:29,260
the first man in the world to teach scuba.

294
00:16:29,261 --> 00:16:32,400
He gave lessons to students in a pool in Paris.

295
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:35,900
In 1933, this Naval captain and brilliant inventor

296
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,300
obtained a patent for his scuba system.

297
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:41,380
It consisted of a compressed air bottle slung on the chest

298
00:16:41,480 --> 00:16:44,280
with an air hose to a full face mask.

299
00:16:44,380 --> 00:16:47,540
He used a hand-operated valve to allow the diver

300
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:50,380
to control the free flow of air.

301
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:55,360
Le Priere founded the first sport diving society in 1934.

302
00:16:55,460 --> 00:16:57,860
His group even performed an underwater ballet

303
00:16:57,861 --> 00:17:01,560
ballet with colored lights at the 1937 Paris Fair.

304
00:17:04,760 --> 00:17:07,300
World War II drove the carefree divers

305
00:17:07,400 --> 00:17:10,900
from the Mediterranean and closed the pools in Paris.

306
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,880
But self-contained diving was to take on monumental importance

307
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:16,120
in combat.

308
00:17:16,220 --> 00:17:19,240
It was top secret on both sides, and the underwater warriors

309
00:17:19,340 --> 00:17:21,980
became some of the bravest fighting men in the military.

310
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:31,100
These classified secret underwater operations

311
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:34,000
were filmed by the US Office of Strategic Service,

312
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:36,660
now known as the OSS.

313
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:40,900
This training film shows a diver using an oxygen rebreather,

314
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,600
driving a British underwater combat vehicle, which

315
00:17:43,700 --> 00:17:46,580
carried him into enemy harbors, where he could attach

316
00:17:46,680 --> 00:17:49,340
an underwater explosive called a limpet mine

317
00:17:49,440 --> 00:17:51,680
onto the hulls of enemy ships.

318
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:55,180
But the British were not the first to use combat divers.

319
00:17:55,181 --> 00:17:57,980
It was the Italians, and first in attacks

320
00:17:58,080 --> 00:18:01,120
against Gibraltar in 1940.

321
00:18:01,220 --> 00:18:04,620
The British called them frogmen for their rubber suits

322
00:18:04,720 --> 00:18:06,620
and frog-like fins.

323
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:10,200
But the Italians were literally human torpedoes.

324
00:18:10,300 --> 00:18:13,160
Wearing oxygen rebreathers, they straddled what

325
00:18:13,260 --> 00:18:16,840
was called a miaule, or pig, and sailed underwater,

326
00:18:16,940 --> 00:18:20,600
manned with a 300-kilogram warhead.

327
00:18:20,700 --> 00:18:22,300
And much like this training film,

328
00:18:22,301 --> 00:18:25,940
they swam alongside the enemy ship, clamped the torpedo

329
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:28,940
to the hull, set the fuse, and swam away.

330
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:38,040
The first Italian attacks mystified the British Navy,

331
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:41,060
penetrating even anti-submarine defenses.

332
00:18:41,160 --> 00:18:43,260
The attacks seemed to come from nowhere.

333
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:46,400
But once British intelligence discovered what they were facing,

334
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:48,960
they quickly fought back.

335
00:18:49,060 --> 00:18:51,100
So what they would do is drop depth charges

336
00:18:51,101 --> 00:18:54,700
depth charges or hand grenades or some kind of explosives.

337
00:18:54,701 --> 00:18:56,800
In a lot of cases, it would actually kill the divers

338
00:18:56,801 --> 00:19:00,140
if they were close enough or just break their eardrums.

339
00:19:00,141 --> 00:19:02,280
And it's a little pain.

340
00:19:02,281 --> 00:19:04,040
The mission's more important.

341
00:19:04,041 --> 00:19:07,220
The Americans were the last to join the Corps of Frogmen,

342
00:19:07,221 --> 00:19:11,080
and it wasn't really until the invasion of the Pacific.

343
00:19:11,081 --> 00:19:13,660
They were 100-man volunteer units named

344
00:19:13,661 --> 00:19:17,200
underwater demolition teams, but they were sometimes called

345
00:19:17,201 --> 00:19:19,260
the naked warriors.

346
00:19:19,261 --> 00:19:23,100
Late to start, they quickly became an unqualified success.

347
00:19:23,101 --> 00:19:26,400
200 US divers carried out missions at Guam,

348
00:19:26,401 --> 00:19:28,300
and not a man was lost.

349
00:19:28,301 --> 00:19:30,740
The first UDTs cleared the way for the Marines

350
00:19:30,741 --> 00:19:33,800
to land beach invasions by blasting reefs and locating

351
00:19:33,801 --> 00:19:36,740
mines, often with the enemy there on the beach shooting

352
00:19:36,741 --> 00:19:37,740
at them.

353
00:19:37,741 --> 00:19:40,380
And they had no equipment except for goggles, a knife,

354
00:19:40,381 --> 00:19:42,920
and a swimsuit.

355
00:19:42,921 --> 00:19:46,120
By the end of the war, frogmen had established themselves

356
00:19:46,121 --> 00:19:49,260
as a proud and vital part of the US Navy.

357
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:51,620
And their importance has only grown.

358
00:19:56,660 --> 00:20:01,260
In 1962, President Kennedy inaugurated the Navy SEALs.

359
00:20:01,360 --> 00:20:04,600
And since then, they have earned the reputation by many

360
00:20:04,700 --> 00:20:08,000
as the best trained underwater warriors in the world.

361
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:10,120
SEALs are trained with every weapon,

362
00:20:10,220 --> 00:20:12,520
not just every American weapon, but every weapon

363
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:13,820
of other countries.

364
00:20:13,821 --> 00:20:16,720
They usually know two or three or four languages.

365
00:20:16,820 --> 00:20:19,420
Their job isn't just to do things in the waters.

366
00:20:19,520 --> 00:20:22,520
They go on land behind enemy lines or in secret places

367
00:20:22,620 --> 00:20:24,160
and do all kind of naughty work.

368
00:20:24,260 --> 00:20:27,700
But war and combat weren't the only use for scuba.

369
00:20:27,800 --> 00:20:29,440
With the invention of the aqualung

370
00:20:29,540 --> 00:20:33,140
came a new sport and a new craze, proving

371
00:20:33,240 --> 00:20:38,920
there's almost nothing divers won't do underwater.

372
00:20:39,020 --> 00:20:42,040
Captain Jacques Cousteau almost died twice,

373
00:20:42,041 --> 00:20:44,600
testing oxygen rebreathers before he finally

374
00:20:44,700 --> 00:20:46,720
invented the aqua line.

375
00:20:46,820 --> 00:20:50,280
Scuba and deep sea diving will return on Modern Marvels.

376
00:20:55,120 --> 00:21:00,360
We now return to scuba and deep sea diving on Modern Marvels.

377
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:03,900
It was the dawn of recreational scuba diving.

378
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,840
Hans Haas became the first person in history

379
00:21:06,940 --> 00:21:11,040
to explore and film the secrets of the open ocean with scuba.

380
00:21:11,041 --> 00:21:13,940
beginning in 1939.

381
00:21:14,040 --> 00:21:16,740
An Austrian adventurer and brilliant young marine

382
00:21:16,840 --> 00:21:21,480
biologist, Haas was a water god and swam with the fish

383
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:24,220
with the mastery of Neptune.

384
00:21:24,320 --> 00:21:26,660
When we look back into diving history,

385
00:21:26,760 --> 00:21:29,660
this is the man that opened the world's eyes.

386
00:21:29,760 --> 00:21:32,460
This is the first underwater filmmaker that's

387
00:21:32,560 --> 00:21:35,200
out there swimming with the fish,

388
00:21:35,300 --> 00:21:36,760
recording their behavior.

389
00:21:36,860 --> 00:21:40,440
He has so many firsts in footage of film, first

390
00:21:40,441 --> 00:21:44,240
first manta rays, first sharks, all these different things.

391
00:21:44,340 --> 00:21:47,940
And he'll be remembered partly for that,

392
00:21:48,040 --> 00:21:50,380
but for the great adventure.

393
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:53,680
Haas donned a rebreather, fins, and a face mask,

394
00:21:53,780 --> 00:21:57,020
and changed walk diving into swim diving,

395
00:21:57,120 --> 00:22:01,800
to study and film a whole new underwater world.

396
00:22:01,900 --> 00:22:03,600
And from the Aegean to the Red Sea,

397
00:22:03,700 --> 00:22:07,560
he led his own expeditions from continent to continent,

398
00:22:07,561 --> 00:22:11,560
totally self-funded, and with an inexhaustible fascination

399
00:22:11,660 --> 00:22:13,100
for his subject.

400
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:15,240
He stirred the mainly European public

401
00:22:15,340 --> 00:22:17,900
with his best-selling books and films.

402
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:21,600
But in America, he was never really well known.

403
00:22:21,700 --> 00:22:24,380
But instead, the man who did become

404
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:26,580
the legend of the underwater world

405
00:22:26,680 --> 00:22:29,880
was Navy Captain Jacques-Yves Cousteau.

406
00:22:29,980 --> 00:22:32,960
While Nazis marched into Paris and ruled his country

407
00:22:33,060 --> 00:22:37,560
with an iron fist, Cousteau and engineer Emile Gagnon

408
00:22:37,561 --> 00:22:40,760
developed the aqualung, and finally gave us a safe way

409
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:45,340
to explore the world Hans Haas had opened for us.

410
00:22:45,440 --> 00:22:48,140
But Cousteau was not really an inventor.

411
00:22:48,240 --> 00:22:51,140
He was an early filmmaker, and his desire

412
00:22:51,240 --> 00:22:54,680
to photograph his friend Frederic Dumas underwater

413
00:22:54,780 --> 00:22:56,440
inspired the invention.

414
00:22:56,540 --> 00:23:01,120
They were doing freediving, and Frederic Dumas was a world

415
00:23:01,220 --> 00:23:03,160
spearfishing champion.

416
00:23:03,161 --> 00:23:07,000
So my dad was following him, mostly to film him.

417
00:23:07,700 --> 00:23:11,300
So they had to synchronize their diving, and it was difficult.

418
00:23:11,480 --> 00:23:13,020
They were going down very deep.

419
00:23:13,180 --> 00:23:18,000
They had to stay there for long periods of times, like two minutes, which is a long time.

420
00:23:18,560 --> 00:23:21,320
And they felt it was tiring.

421
00:23:21,321 --> 00:23:29,180
And that's when they started thinking about, how can we stay there and be like a fish?

422
00:23:29,181 --> 00:23:33,180
What Cousteau and Guignan did was modify the Rukai Roll

423
00:23:33,280 --> 00:23:36,780
Denneru's regulator and improve upon the air hose

424
00:23:36,880 --> 00:23:38,220
and mouthpiece.

425
00:23:38,320 --> 00:23:41,360
They ended up with a fully automatic aqualung,

426
00:23:41,460 --> 00:23:45,160
which supplied air upon demand to the diver at exactly

427
00:23:45,260 --> 00:23:47,500
the appropriate pressure.

428
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:51,460
The Cousteau-Guignan aqualung became the most important diving

429
00:23:51,560 --> 00:23:53,200
tool of the century.

430
00:23:53,300 --> 00:23:56,340
For the first time in history, the aqualung allowed divers

431
00:23:56,440 --> 00:23:58,880
to reach depths they could never reach before,

432
00:23:58,881 --> 00:24:00,480
and stay down longer.

433
00:24:00,580 --> 00:24:04,380
It was a passport to inner space.

434
00:24:04,480 --> 00:24:07,760
But as a naval officer, Cousteau was not a rich man.

435
00:24:07,860 --> 00:24:10,960
And equipment and cameras and film were expensive.

436
00:24:11,060 --> 00:24:13,220
He had to cut corners whenever possible

437
00:24:13,320 --> 00:24:16,060
to film his new world.

438
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:19,360
And I remember my mother putting her jewelry

439
00:24:19,460 --> 00:24:22,880
at the pawn shop in order for my dad

440
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:24,880
to do what he wanted to do.

441
00:24:24,980 --> 00:24:28,880
And I also remember during the war, and I was tiny,

442
00:24:28,881 --> 00:24:34,280
my mother spending hours under the blanket in the bed

443
00:24:34,281 --> 00:24:39,140
gluing pieces of film together

444
00:24:39,141 --> 00:24:41,980
because you couldn't find rolls of film

445
00:24:41,981 --> 00:24:44,960
to put in the movie camera.

446
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:47,880
So they would take still rolls of films,

447
00:24:48,240 --> 00:24:49,720
which had 36 frames,

448
00:24:49,900 --> 00:24:52,100
and she would glue them together

449
00:24:52,101 --> 00:24:53,520
in order to make a roll of film.

450
00:24:54,060 --> 00:24:55,220
More than anything,

451
00:24:55,221 --> 00:24:57,380
Cousteau was a passionate explorer

452
00:24:57,381 --> 00:25:00,220
with a mission to share his discoveries.

453
00:25:00,320 --> 00:25:02,680
And his films did just that.

454
00:25:02,780 --> 00:25:04,880
They sparked an interest in a new sport that

455
00:25:04,980 --> 00:25:08,720
has never stopped growing.

456
00:25:08,820 --> 00:25:11,700
And in the early 1950s, Southern California

457
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:13,700
was more than ready for scuba.

458
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:15,700
In fact, it had been waiting.

459
00:25:15,800 --> 00:25:18,560
Surfers were already becoming spear fishermen,

460
00:25:18,660 --> 00:25:20,200
but without a tank.

461
00:25:20,300 --> 00:25:21,640
I was a surfer.

462
00:25:21,740 --> 00:25:25,500
And I saw these people in the water,

463
00:25:25,600 --> 00:25:26,980
and they were coming up with things.

464
00:25:26,981 --> 00:25:30,360
and I paddled over there and I said, what are you guys doing?

465
00:25:30,900 --> 00:25:31,920
We're getting dinner.

466
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:34,600
And that made a lot of sense to me.

467
00:25:35,080 --> 00:25:38,160
And so my initial lure was dinner.

468
00:25:38,900 --> 00:25:43,940
Cousteau's aqualung didn't hit a California sports shop until 1952,

469
00:25:44,420 --> 00:25:48,980
and by then spear fishermen had already read in popular science how to build their own.

470
00:25:48,981 --> 00:25:53,380
We built a couple of our own lungs artificial breathing apparatus

471
00:25:53,381 --> 00:25:57,480
from World War II bomber oxygen kits.

472
00:25:57,580 --> 00:26:00,420
We'd convert them over and make them work underwater.

473
00:26:00,520 --> 00:26:03,860
But they were always haphazard at best.

474
00:26:03,960 --> 00:26:07,020
And good thing we knew how to hold our breath and swim

475
00:26:07,120 --> 00:26:09,000
and that sort of thing.

476
00:26:09,100 --> 00:26:10,960
If that surface too high for you,

477
00:26:11,060 --> 00:26:14,560
you have to make a judgment factor as to whether or not

478
00:26:14,660 --> 00:26:17,380
you can handle that particular surface.

479
00:26:17,480 --> 00:26:21,140
But when Cousteau's aqualung finally did become available,

480
00:26:21,141 --> 00:26:23,740
It was a piece of gear that really worked.

481
00:26:23,840 --> 00:26:25,440
You didn't have to worry about it.

482
00:26:25,540 --> 00:26:27,780
You could go diving three or four times a day,

483
00:26:27,880 --> 00:26:29,780
and it worked every time.

484
00:26:29,880 --> 00:26:33,380
And single-handedly, it started a craze.

485
00:26:33,480 --> 00:26:34,280
People would do anything.

486
00:26:34,380 --> 00:26:37,120
They'd dive in quarries, in rivers, anything,

487
00:26:37,220 --> 00:26:38,320
just to be in the water.

488
00:26:38,420 --> 00:26:40,320
So a lot of people dove for years

489
00:26:40,420 --> 00:26:43,220
before they ever went to the sea or went to the Caribbean.

490
00:26:43,320 --> 00:26:45,000
Once they strapped on an aqualon,

491
00:26:45,100 --> 00:26:47,940
divers did the darndest things.

492
00:26:48,040 --> 00:26:50,740
First, the clubs formed, bottom scratchers,

493
00:26:50,741 --> 00:26:53,380
sea fools, mud sharks, hundreds of them,

494
00:26:53,480 --> 00:26:56,380
and they cavorted underwater, playing games

495
00:26:56,480 --> 00:26:59,220
and submerging themselves in the giddy new excitement

496
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:02,480
of their new underwater playground.

497
00:27:02,580 --> 00:27:04,680
Some gave up their weapons for a camera,

498
00:27:04,780 --> 00:27:07,960
preferring to shoot fish with film instead of a spear gun.

499
00:27:08,060 --> 00:27:11,860
And others, who lived hundreds of miles from the closest ocean,

500
00:27:11,960 --> 00:27:14,760
braved the cold and dove in icy lakes.

501
00:27:14,761 --> 00:27:20,660
And if the frigid pleasures of ice diving weren't dangerous

502
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:24,440
enough, scuba divers discovered another pursuit that

503
00:27:24,540 --> 00:27:26,340
was downright deadly.

504
00:27:26,440 --> 00:27:29,380
They began to explore underwater caves,

505
00:27:29,480 --> 00:27:31,880
and some never returned.

506
00:27:31,980 --> 00:27:35,880
Cave diving was the most dangerous aspect of the new sport.

507
00:27:35,980 --> 00:27:38,020
It has probably killed more divers

508
00:27:38,120 --> 00:27:41,380
than all the rest of diving combined.

509
00:27:41,381 --> 00:27:44,280
But there were those who had a more serious interest

510
00:27:44,380 --> 00:27:48,180
in the new aqualung, scientists.

511
00:27:48,280 --> 00:27:51,020
Underwater archaeologists and marine biologists

512
00:27:51,120 --> 00:27:53,360
were now putting on a tank and taking

513
00:27:53,460 --> 00:27:55,500
their laboratories underwater.

514
00:27:55,600 --> 00:27:59,460
As it was said, it was easier to turn a scientist into a diver

515
00:27:59,560 --> 00:28:02,400
than a diver into a scientist.

516
00:28:02,500 --> 00:28:07,940
It was such a joy for me to see with my own eyes.

517
00:28:07,941 --> 00:28:11,940
I've seen thousands of pickled fish in museums.

518
00:28:11,941 --> 00:28:15,940
There's nothing like actually having them come to look at you.

519
00:28:15,941 --> 00:28:19,940
Celebrated oceanographer and scientist Dr. Sylvia Earle

520
00:28:19,941 --> 00:28:23,940
was one of the first marine biologists to use scuba in her studies.

521
00:28:23,941 --> 00:28:27,940
Before the aqualung, she had to use dredging equipment or nets

522
00:28:27,941 --> 00:28:29,961
to find and study her specimens.

523
00:28:29,962 --> 00:28:33,940
Just imagine that you're an alien up in the sky, dragging a net

524
00:28:33,941 --> 00:28:37,440
through the streets, catching pedestrians,

525
00:28:37,441 --> 00:28:40,440
chunks of shrubbery, bits of this, bits of that.

526
00:28:40,441 --> 00:28:44,440
Squish it all together and dump it out on the deck of your ship

527
00:28:44,441 --> 00:28:47,940
up in the sky and try to figure out what's going on down there.

528
00:28:47,941 --> 00:28:53,940
Imagine taking the census of the United States using a net.

529
00:28:53,941 --> 00:28:56,940
But scientists weren't content with their newfound ability

530
00:28:56,941 --> 00:28:58,940
to study the sea.

531
00:28:58,941 --> 00:29:02,940
Now they wanted to stay down longer and go much deeper.

532
00:29:02,941 --> 00:29:05,480
And so a new breed of diver was born,

533
00:29:05,580 --> 00:29:09,580
including a famous astronaut who turned aquanaut

534
00:29:09,680 --> 00:29:12,020
to learn how to live underwater.

535
00:29:15,260 --> 00:29:18,860
The average American scuba diver today is 24 years old

536
00:29:18,960 --> 00:29:22,760
and earns an income of around $35,000.

537
00:29:22,860 --> 00:29:26,600
Scuba and deep sea diving will return on Modern Marvels.

538
00:29:26,601 --> 00:29:27,500
Marvels.

539
00:29:31,400 --> 00:29:37,340
We now return to scuba and deep sea diving on modern marvels.

540
00:29:37,440 --> 00:29:39,540
It was August of 1938.

541
00:29:39,640 --> 00:29:42,580
After 11 years of research, a hardhat diver

542
00:29:42,680 --> 00:29:47,760
bravely attempted a new world record, a 500-foot dive.

543
00:29:47,860 --> 00:29:50,360
But as he descended, he didn't breathe air.

544
00:29:50,460 --> 00:29:52,860
He tried the Navy's new recipe.

545
00:29:52,960 --> 00:29:56,600
Oxygen and nitrogen was replaced with oxygen and helium.

546
00:29:56,601 --> 00:29:59,140
On the bottom, 500 feet, Mattis.

547
00:29:59,240 --> 00:30:00,200
How do you feel?

548
00:30:00,300 --> 00:30:01,740
I'm fine.

549
00:30:01,840 --> 00:30:03,040
OK.

550
00:30:03,140 --> 00:30:06,240
The 500-foot plunge was a total success

551
00:30:06,340 --> 00:30:09,240
and a breakthrough in the exploration of the deep.

552
00:30:09,340 --> 00:30:12,780
Until then, divers could not descend much past 200 feet

553
00:30:12,880 --> 00:30:16,360
without suffering a strange phenomenon called nitrogen

554
00:30:16,460 --> 00:30:19,320
narcosis, or rapture of the deep.

555
00:30:19,420 --> 00:30:22,200
The high concentration of nitrogen in their bodies

556
00:30:22,300 --> 00:30:24,960
had a narcotic effect and rendered some senseless

557
00:30:25,060 --> 00:30:26,600
to danger.

558
00:30:26,601 --> 00:30:28,840
Everybody is different.

559
00:30:29,660 --> 00:30:33,940
And some individuals can't think very clearly at 170 feet.

560
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:37,200
Some can think clear as a bell down to 220.

561
00:30:37,900 --> 00:30:42,580
But somewhere between 150 and 220,

562
00:30:43,180 --> 00:30:45,880
everybody gets kind of stupid.

563
00:30:46,480 --> 00:30:48,500
America led in the helium studies

564
00:30:48,501 --> 00:30:51,320
since it had the only helium supply in the world.

565
00:30:51,321 --> 00:30:53,320
But it was limited and tightly controlled,

566
00:30:54,400 --> 00:30:56,280
and scientists worried they'd run out.

567
00:30:56,600 --> 00:30:59,340
There were times when we were watching helium supplies

568
00:30:59,440 --> 00:31:02,840
dwindling where we began to panic when we saw balloons

569
00:31:02,940 --> 00:31:07,980
at parties with helium, thinking maybe we should have saved

570
00:31:08,080 --> 00:31:08,880
that.

571
00:31:08,980 --> 00:31:11,080
But the discovery of the oxygen helium

572
00:31:11,180 --> 00:31:14,380
cocktail didn't solve the problem of the bends.

573
00:31:14,480 --> 00:31:16,980
Although divers could now go down to 200 feet

574
00:31:17,080 --> 00:31:19,860
without becoming intoxicated, they still

575
00:31:19,960 --> 00:31:22,800
couldn't stay down long without tedious and time

576
00:31:22,900 --> 00:31:25,300
consuming decompression.

577
00:31:25,301 --> 00:31:30,300
Then Navy surgeon George Bond discovered saturation.

578
00:31:30,400 --> 00:31:32,300
After a seven-year study, he learned

579
00:31:32,400 --> 00:31:35,540
that once a diver's body absorbed all the nitrogen or helium

580
00:31:35,640 --> 00:31:40,040
it could, then decompression time no longer increased.

581
00:31:40,140 --> 00:31:42,960
It made no difference if the diver stayed down a day,

582
00:31:43,060 --> 00:31:45,360
a week, or a month.

583
00:31:45,460 --> 00:31:48,660
So now all a diver needed was an underwater house,

584
00:31:48,760 --> 00:31:52,400
pressurized to his depth, and he could live underwater.

585
00:31:52,401 --> 00:31:57,200
And that's just what the Sea Labs became.

586
00:31:57,300 --> 00:32:01,540
In the mid-1960s, the US Navy built three underwater habitats

587
00:32:01,640 --> 00:32:06,780
for a new breed of diver called the Aquanauts, the most famous

588
00:32:06,880 --> 00:32:09,020
astronaut Scott Carpenter.

589
00:32:09,120 --> 00:32:12,580
In Sea Lab 2, Carpenter and his team of 10 men

590
00:32:12,680 --> 00:32:16,960
moved to their cigar-shaped home, 205 feet down,

591
00:32:17,060 --> 00:32:19,600
off the coast of La Jolla, California.

592
00:32:19,601 --> 00:32:23,740
Living there for 45 days, they cooked their own food,

593
00:32:23,840 --> 00:32:27,740
underwent tests, and had daily chores on the ocean bottom.

594
00:32:27,840 --> 00:32:30,340
They described their working relationship

595
00:32:30,440 --> 00:32:33,340
as walking on eggs.

596
00:32:33,440 --> 00:32:34,640
They were down there.

597
00:32:34,740 --> 00:32:37,160
They couldn't get out.

598
00:32:37,260 --> 00:32:42,260
So they were very, very careful not to irritate each other.

599
00:32:42,360 --> 00:32:44,500
Another problem C-Lab had to contend with

600
00:32:44,600 --> 00:32:47,800
was what doctors laughingly called Swahelium.

601
00:32:47,801 --> 00:32:50,740
If you breathe helium, you sound like Donald Duck.

602
00:32:50,840 --> 00:32:54,640
And the Navy had to invent an unscrambler to decipher it.

603
00:32:54,740 --> 00:32:58,140
Anderson, man the helium unscrambler

604
00:32:58,240 --> 00:33:01,740
and give me some test phrases.

605
00:33:01,840 --> 00:33:06,660
This is C-Lab, land and ready.

606
00:33:06,760 --> 00:33:07,560
Are you on?

607
00:33:07,660 --> 00:33:09,960
Let's have the test phrases now, please.

608
00:33:10,060 --> 00:33:11,160
I'm on.

609
00:33:11,260 --> 00:33:13,360
Hey, you all, come in, sir.

610
00:33:13,460 --> 00:33:17,100
So successful was C-Lab II that in 1970,

611
00:33:17,101 --> 00:33:21,100
In 1970, the government launched a new underwater habitat program,

612
00:33:21,200 --> 00:33:25,100
Tektite II, but this one started a controversy.

613
00:33:30,740 --> 00:33:33,580
And when applications came in from volunteers,

614
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:36,160
the Tektite II leaders had a problem.

615
00:33:36,260 --> 00:33:38,260
Many of the candidates were women.

616
00:33:38,360 --> 00:33:41,320
And how would women live with men underwater?

617
00:33:41,420 --> 00:33:46,160
They had to decide, will there or will there not be women included?

618
00:33:46,161 --> 00:33:50,860
The initial reaction was not only no, it was hell no.

619
00:33:50,861 --> 00:33:53,260
But Dr. Jim Miller, head of the program,

620
00:33:53,261 --> 00:33:56,200
finally gave in with the decision that the women would have

621
00:33:56,201 --> 00:33:59,240
to live together without the man.

622
00:33:59,241 --> 00:34:02,080
And so five women, including Sylvia Earle,

623
00:34:02,081 --> 00:34:05,880
were chosen to go and headed for the Virgin Islands.

624
00:34:05,881 --> 00:34:09,820
The female scientists were to perform the same jobs as the men.

625
00:34:09,821 --> 00:34:12,880
Some of the press, at least, never saw them as equals.

626
00:34:12,881 --> 00:34:15,920
We were rarely called aquanauts.

627
00:34:15,921 --> 00:34:17,620
We were called aqua bells.

628
00:34:17,621 --> 00:34:20,860
We were called aqua babes, aqua maids, aqua chicks,

629
00:34:20,861 --> 00:34:21,960
even aqua naughties.

630
00:34:21,961 --> 00:34:23,200
I love that one.

631
00:34:23,201 --> 00:34:25,360
But Tektai II, and more importantly,

632
00:34:25,361 --> 00:34:28,600
the CLAB projects not only proved that men and women

633
00:34:28,601 --> 00:34:31,460
could live in the deep, more importantly,

634
00:34:31,461 --> 00:34:33,200
they could work there too.

635
00:34:33,201 --> 00:34:35,540
And using this knowledge, the oil industry

636
00:34:35,541 --> 00:34:37,940
began to conduct experiments of its own

637
00:34:37,941 --> 00:34:39,440
to drill for a new fortune.

638
00:34:39,441 --> 00:34:44,440
Running on empty.

639
00:34:44,540 --> 00:34:48,180
The early 1970s saw us waiting in long lines

640
00:34:48,280 --> 00:34:50,820
for short supplies of gasoline.

641
00:34:50,920 --> 00:34:54,520
With the Mideast oil embargo starting in 1973,

642
00:34:54,620 --> 00:34:58,460
America was just learning how important a role energy played

643
00:34:58,560 --> 00:34:59,700
in the good life.

644
00:34:59,800 --> 00:35:02,800
Immediately, oil companies raced to begin drilling

645
00:35:02,900 --> 00:35:04,400
in the open seas.

646
00:35:04,500 --> 00:35:07,860
They wanted thousands of divers, and they wanted them now.

647
00:35:07,861 --> 00:35:11,600
and they wanted them to dive as deep as they needed.

648
00:35:11,700 --> 00:35:14,500
Sheer, unadulterated greed.

649
00:35:14,600 --> 00:35:17,500
They were saying, we're going into 700 feet of water.

650
00:35:17,600 --> 00:35:20,080
If you can't go there, you're going to lose the contract

651
00:35:20,180 --> 00:35:21,400
and we'll use something else.

652
00:35:21,500 --> 00:35:22,680
We said, no, no, we'll go.

653
00:35:22,780 --> 00:35:23,780
We'll be there.

654
00:35:23,880 --> 00:35:25,620
And we had no idea how the hell we were going to do it.

655
00:35:25,720 --> 00:35:27,340
The guys were falling like flies.

656
00:35:27,440 --> 00:35:29,780
In the North Sea, for instance, in one season,

657
00:35:29,880 --> 00:35:32,180
there were, you know, gosh, a double handful of guys

658
00:35:32,280 --> 00:35:33,780
that bit the dust.

659
00:35:33,880 --> 00:35:36,580
Offshore oil rigs created a golden age

660
00:35:36,581 --> 00:35:38,080
of commercial diving.

661
00:35:38,180 --> 00:35:41,380
Having already helped build our nation's ports and harbors

662
00:35:41,480 --> 00:35:45,320
and bridges and dams, and repair and salvage our ships,

663
00:35:45,420 --> 00:35:50,300
hardhat divers were now to become underwater engineers.

664
00:35:50,400 --> 00:35:53,300
They were already the safest divers in the world

665
00:35:53,400 --> 00:35:56,100
because of a continuous air supply and communication

666
00:35:56,200 --> 00:35:59,700
from the surface, but now they were to become the richest.

667
00:35:59,800 --> 00:36:03,800
These men were paid by the foot, depending on how deep they went,

668
00:36:03,801 --> 00:36:06,560
they went, and they were men who were willing to spend long

669
00:36:06,660 --> 00:36:10,640
and seemingly endless idle days in decompression chambers.

670
00:36:10,740 --> 00:36:14,640
Of course, there was one thing that made it worthwhile.

671
00:36:14,740 --> 00:36:16,240
The money was obscene.

672
00:36:16,340 --> 00:36:19,240
There's no other word for it, absolutely obscene.

673
00:36:19,340 --> 00:36:21,360
We were making money hand over fist.

674
00:36:21,460 --> 00:36:25,660
During the oil rush, you could make $2,500 a day.

675
00:36:25,760 --> 00:36:28,200
A man could buy a house in a month.

676
00:36:28,300 --> 00:36:31,160
But it was harsh, rugged work.

677
00:36:31,260 --> 00:36:32,860
The only time you're not going to go on the water

678
00:36:32,861 --> 00:36:34,660
is maybe if you're going to get killed.

679
00:36:34,760 --> 00:36:37,160
If there's any way of getting a job done,

680
00:36:37,260 --> 00:36:40,100
they expect you to get in the water and do the work.

681
00:36:40,200 --> 00:36:43,980
But the oil industry didn't like paying rock star wages,

682
00:36:44,080 --> 00:36:47,040
and they'd rather not risk lives to go deep.

683
00:36:47,140 --> 00:36:50,780
They just had to find a new way to tend their rigs.

684
00:36:50,880 --> 00:36:54,880
They turned to Pop Perez and his walking refrigerator,

685
00:36:54,980 --> 00:36:59,920
ushering in the age of the one atmosphere suit.

686
00:36:59,921 --> 00:37:02,460
On Sea Lab 2, Tuffy, a trained porpoise,

687
00:37:02,560 --> 00:37:06,120
acted as the crew's mailman, delivering their packages

688
00:37:06,220 --> 00:37:08,420
to and from the surface.

689
00:37:08,520 --> 00:37:10,160
Modern Marvels will return.

690
00:37:14,360 --> 00:37:16,740
We now return to Modern Marvels.

691
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:23,680
It's almost impossible to see the difference

692
00:37:23,780 --> 00:37:28,680
between this aquanaut's gym suit and an astronaut suit.

693
00:37:28,681 --> 00:37:32,320
They have so much in common, just like the worlds they fly

694
00:37:32,420 --> 00:37:33,520
and swim in.

695
00:37:33,620 --> 00:37:35,780
In fact, training for work in outer space

696
00:37:35,880 --> 00:37:38,060
is so similar to work in inner space

697
00:37:38,160 --> 00:37:40,620
that you cannot be an astronaut unless you first

698
00:37:40,720 --> 00:37:42,520
become a scuba diver.

699
00:37:42,620 --> 00:37:46,160
The astronauts all work in zero gravity simulators, which

700
00:37:46,260 --> 00:37:48,300
are nothing more than water-filled tanks.

701
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:49,960
And they're surrounded by scuba divers

702
00:37:50,060 --> 00:37:51,440
and they go through their paces.

703
00:37:51,540 --> 00:37:53,500
So whether it's on the bottom or whether it's in space

704
00:37:53,600 --> 00:37:54,700
makes very little difference.

705
00:37:54,800 --> 00:37:57,680
The life support systems, the tethers, the umbilical,

706
00:37:57,681 --> 00:38:00,520
the communication, all of the technology that goes into it

707
00:38:00,620 --> 00:38:02,120
is remarkably similar.

708
00:38:02,220 --> 00:38:04,020
But this space age looking suit

709
00:38:04,120 --> 00:38:08,500
is for underwater work only, deep underwater work.

710
00:38:08,600 --> 00:38:12,500
And believe it or not, it's actually 70 years old.

711
00:38:12,600 --> 00:38:19,300
And its long history goes back to 1715, to a man and his barrel.

712
00:38:19,400 --> 00:38:22,540
When Englishman John Lethbridge obtained his patent,

713
00:38:22,640 --> 00:38:25,180
he called his invention a diving machine.

714
00:38:25,181 --> 00:38:29,020
But it was really a wooden barrel, a barrel with armholes

715
00:38:29,120 --> 00:38:33,180
and a sight glass that had air pumped down to it with bellows.

716
00:38:33,280 --> 00:38:36,200
Crude, but a success.

717
00:38:36,300 --> 00:38:39,860
That invention was the first atmospheric diving suit.

718
00:38:39,960 --> 00:38:42,100
And it wouldn't be until the 1920s

719
00:38:42,200 --> 00:38:45,940
that the idea would again resurface, and this time,

720
00:38:46,040 --> 00:38:47,640
in armor.

721
00:38:47,740 --> 00:38:51,240
The idea was to build a suit so strong that it could withstand

722
00:38:51,340 --> 00:38:53,040
great pressure.

723
00:38:53,041 --> 00:38:56,480
So the diver inside could breathe at normal surface air,

724
00:38:56,580 --> 00:38:59,480
no bends, no decompression.

725
00:38:59,580 --> 00:39:03,780
But they were heavy, bulky, and expensive.

726
00:39:03,880 --> 00:39:07,820
And it wasn't until Joseph Pop Perez invented his

727
00:39:07,920 --> 00:39:12,200
that the dream of deep diving was realized.

728
00:39:12,300 --> 00:39:15,060
Raised in the Middle East but living in England,

729
00:39:15,160 --> 00:39:18,760
Pop invented his deep diving armor out of magnesium

730
00:39:18,860 --> 00:39:22,040
with articulated joints filled with oil.

731
00:39:22,041 --> 00:39:27,180
It performed perfectly, but nobody wanted it, not even the Navy.

732
00:39:27,280 --> 00:39:29,540
But it did make some headlines.

733
00:39:29,640 --> 00:39:33,580
In the early 1930s, Perez's assistant, Jim Jarrett,

734
00:39:33,680 --> 00:39:36,760
wore the atmospheric suit for its second deep water dive

735
00:39:36,860 --> 00:39:39,500
to locate the sunken ship, Lusitania,

736
00:39:39,600 --> 00:39:41,260
off the coast of Ireland.

737
00:39:41,360 --> 00:39:44,320
But after the dive, the suit was nicknamed Jim,

738
00:39:44,420 --> 00:39:47,700
and then retired, or so Pop thought.

739
00:39:47,800 --> 00:39:51,800
30 years later, deep water oil exploitation in the North Sea

740
00:39:51,801 --> 00:39:55,240
was booming, and the race was on to develop a working

741
00:39:55,340 --> 00:39:57,040
atmospheric suit.

742
00:39:57,140 --> 00:40:00,780
Two engineers heard about Pat Perez in an English pub,

743
00:40:00,880 --> 00:40:03,640
looked him up, and asked about Jim.

744
00:40:03,740 --> 00:40:07,020
They found the suit, and it was actually in a chicken coop

745
00:40:07,120 --> 00:40:09,820
and covered with chicken manure, or whatever you call it,

746
00:40:09,920 --> 00:40:12,220
and straw, et cetera.

747
00:40:12,320 --> 00:40:14,720
And it was in a big gray box, and on the side of the box

748
00:40:14,820 --> 00:40:16,800
it said, Lusitania Diving Engine.

749
00:40:16,900 --> 00:40:19,360
So boy, talk about going back in history.

750
00:40:19,460 --> 00:40:21,200
The suit was dusted off and tested

751
00:40:21,201 --> 00:40:23,840
by the 80-year-old Perez himself.

752
00:40:23,940 --> 00:40:28,400
It took three hours to get the suit off of Pop, but it worked.

753
00:40:28,500 --> 00:40:31,280
And after modifications by the 1980s,

754
00:40:31,380 --> 00:40:35,240
it was used around the world and spawned a new technology

755
00:40:35,340 --> 00:40:37,720
of one-atmosphere diving.

756
00:40:37,820 --> 00:40:40,080
But even Jim wasn't enough.

757
00:40:40,180 --> 00:40:43,360
Oil workers wanted a better, lighter, more mobile suit

758
00:40:43,460 --> 00:40:45,060
than Jim could ever offer.

759
00:40:45,160 --> 00:40:48,700
And so the wasp was invented.

760
00:40:48,701 --> 00:40:50,940
Laughingly called the meat in a can,

761
00:40:51,040 --> 00:40:54,880
the wasp was really a flying gym suit with thrusters on it,

762
00:40:54,980 --> 00:40:57,440
although helpful it had its limitations.

763
00:40:57,540 --> 00:41:00,440
It had a tether, it wasn't free swimming,

764
00:41:00,540 --> 00:41:03,140
and the arms were very limited in movement.

765
00:41:03,240 --> 00:41:07,460
So its inventor, Phil Newton, went back to the drawing board.

766
00:41:07,560 --> 00:41:10,760
PHIL NEWTON, It took me five years of knuckle bruising work

767
00:41:10,860 --> 00:41:12,460
and test after test.

768
00:41:12,560 --> 00:41:14,060
And the only reason that I kept up with it

769
00:41:14,160 --> 00:41:15,800
was because I'd invested so much money in it,

770
00:41:15,900 --> 00:41:18,460
and I shot my mouth off so much that I was embarrassed

771
00:41:18,461 --> 00:41:21,020
I was embarrassed not to succeed.

772
00:41:21,120 --> 00:41:25,360
And so the new suit was made.

773
00:41:25,460 --> 00:41:28,000
It could dive down to 2,000 feet.

774
00:41:28,100 --> 00:41:33,800
And most importantly, it had full articulation.

775
00:41:33,900 --> 00:41:39,480
But even a new suit can't go down 12,640 feet deep,

776
00:41:39,580 --> 00:41:42,780
where the Titanic rests in its watery grave.

777
00:41:42,880 --> 00:41:46,660
The crushing power of water at that depth is staggering.

778
00:41:46,661 --> 00:41:50,460
And the only human way possible to capture its images

779
00:41:50,461 --> 00:41:53,260
is not with a human.

780
00:41:53,261 --> 00:41:55,940
Filmmakers inside Russian submarines

781
00:41:55,941 --> 00:42:00,340
and ROVs remotely operated vehicles equipped with cameras

782
00:42:00,341 --> 00:42:03,240
captured these astonishing images.

783
00:42:03,241 --> 00:42:06,880
From within the subs, operators were able to focus, zoom, pan,

784
00:42:06,881 --> 00:42:11,860
and tilt the ROV cameras and venture further inside the heart

785
00:42:11,861 --> 00:42:16,160
of the Titanic than ever possible before.

786
00:42:16,161 --> 00:42:22,400
And ROVs have also replaced some of the commercial divers' work on oil rigs and on shipwrecks

787
00:42:22,401 --> 00:42:23,400
hiding fortunes.

788
00:42:23,401 --> 00:42:28,720
They're cheaper, they can work 24 hours a day, and they're easy to use.

789
00:42:28,721 --> 00:42:32,860
I mean, if you could play Nintendo, that's what an ROV is.

790
00:42:32,861 --> 00:42:37,580
You put it in the water and you just sit there watching a television camera and telling it

791
00:42:37,581 --> 00:42:38,580
where to go.

792
00:42:38,581 --> 00:42:43,160
It's just a matter of driving it up and down sideways and pressing a button.

793
00:42:43,161 --> 00:42:46,260
But the remotely operated vehicles haven't replaced humans

794
00:42:46,360 --> 00:42:47,360
in the water.

795
00:42:47,460 --> 00:42:51,400
ROVs can find problems on an underwater oil rig,

796
00:42:51,500 --> 00:42:54,780
but often it's the diver who has to fix it.

797
00:42:54,880 --> 00:42:57,140
But with all of our underwater tools,

798
00:42:57,240 --> 00:43:00,580
we still haven't solved our problem of how to go deeper

799
00:43:00,680 --> 00:43:03,320
and stay longer.

800
00:43:03,420 --> 00:43:05,880
Although Alliance Pharmaceutical Corporation

801
00:43:05,980 --> 00:43:08,760
may have come the closest to finding an answer,

802
00:43:08,860 --> 00:43:11,680
with their mixture called Liquivant.

803
00:43:11,681 --> 00:43:14,120
This company, along with several universities,

804
00:43:14,220 --> 00:43:17,560
began research to see if man could breathe liquid.

805
00:43:17,660 --> 00:43:21,360
After all, fetuses breathe fluid for nine months before birth.

806
00:43:21,460 --> 00:43:26,460
And some tantalizing experiments used mice and dogs

807
00:43:26,560 --> 00:43:29,100
and even a man.

808
00:43:29,200 --> 00:43:32,160
They actually cuffed off one lung and filled it

809
00:43:32,260 --> 00:43:36,380
with an oxygenated fluorocarbon and then

810
00:43:36,480 --> 00:43:37,700
sealed off the other lung.

811
00:43:37,800 --> 00:43:39,740
So he was breathing on the wet lung.

812
00:43:39,741 --> 00:43:42,080
And he said it was very strange.

813
00:43:42,180 --> 00:43:43,240
It actually pulled.

814
00:43:43,340 --> 00:43:44,180
You know, he could feel.

815
00:43:44,280 --> 00:43:45,380
So for the first time in his life,

816
00:43:45,480 --> 00:43:48,480
he was aware that there was something in his chest alive.

817
00:43:48,580 --> 00:43:50,280
And he could actually feel as he moved,

818
00:43:50,380 --> 00:43:52,280
feel this thing pulling and twisting.

819
00:43:52,380 --> 00:43:53,980
It was very uncomfortable.

820
00:43:54,080 --> 00:43:56,260
At the same time, American scientists

821
00:43:56,360 --> 00:43:59,960
were working on a mechanical gill that would extract oxygen

822
00:44:00,060 --> 00:44:01,020
from water.

823
00:44:01,120 --> 00:44:04,700
But the Japanese say they now have a version that in 10 years

824
00:44:04,800 --> 00:44:08,160
will be worn like an aqualung and allow you to die for as long

825
00:44:08,161 --> 00:44:10,660
as you like in shallow water.

826
00:44:10,760 --> 00:44:13,360
Here in the United States, manufacturers recently

827
00:44:13,460 --> 00:44:15,300
brought back the rebreather.

828
00:44:15,400 --> 00:44:17,900
This time, instead of using pure oxygen,

829
00:44:18,000 --> 00:44:21,600
it contains a special mixture of oxygen and nitrogen.

830
00:44:21,700 --> 00:44:25,280
So divers can go down deeper than 30 feet and stay down

831
00:44:25,380 --> 00:44:27,540
longer with no bubbles.

832
00:44:27,640 --> 00:44:30,520
But it isn't the answer to the abyss.

833
00:44:30,620 --> 00:44:35,820
In the 1960s, many began to plan for underwater colonies,

834
00:44:35,920 --> 00:44:37,460
but they were never built.

835
00:44:37,461 --> 00:44:40,400
We didn't know then about all the dangers and difficulties

836
00:44:40,500 --> 00:44:44,500
and expense of going and staying down deep.

837
00:44:44,600 --> 00:44:48,140
But today, many again believe those underwater cities

838
00:44:48,240 --> 00:44:49,880
aren't far away.

839
00:44:49,980 --> 00:44:52,680
There will be colonies, cities under the sea,

840
00:44:52,780 --> 00:44:55,620
where you'll talk about a trip to the surface the same way we

841
00:44:55,720 --> 00:44:57,880
now talk about, while we will talk soon,

842
00:44:57,980 --> 00:44:59,380
about a trip into space.

843
00:44:59,480 --> 00:45:02,680
Because it would be such an exciting thing to go and visit,

844
00:45:02,780 --> 00:45:05,180
you know, the land where we came from as opposed to the land

845
00:45:05,280 --> 00:45:06,720
where we live.

846
00:45:06,721 --> 00:45:10,160
Some of us may live to see these visions of a future world

847
00:45:10,260 --> 00:45:11,520
come true.

848
00:45:11,620 --> 00:45:13,720
But more importantly, we must continue

849
00:45:13,820 --> 00:45:16,660
to use our underwater breathing tools

850
00:45:16,760 --> 00:45:21,200
to follow that siren song, that ancient lure of the sea,

851
00:45:21,300 --> 00:45:24,980
discover more about the underwater world we've just

852
00:45:25,080 --> 00:45:27,680
begun to explore.

853
00:45:27,780 --> 00:45:29,480
What's stopping us?

854
00:45:29,580 --> 00:45:33,020
Nothing but our own imagination, nothing

855
00:45:33,021 --> 00:45:36,860
but our understanding that we need to do this,

856
00:45:36,861 --> 00:45:40,860
that it's our survival that's at risk if we don't do this.

857
00:45:40,861 --> 00:45:43,860
We must understand the ocean.

